thread:
maria: FYI hitting a bunch of reports that our npm dual-pkg layout causes runtime errors in Cloudflare Workers / edge bundlers when customers import the ESM build. Anyone seen similar? :thinking:
tony: Yep saw one earlier. Edge bundlers prefer the `exports` conditional mapping but some older bundlers still resolve `main`/`module` weirdly. Do we have the exact error?
maria: stack from user: `Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: Object prototype may only be an Object or null: undefined` when doing `import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference'` in a worker. Happens after tree-shaking.
sam: sounds like they pulled the CJS entry somehow, or the bundler injected `__proto__` mutation. Could be because our `exports` has require fallback first. Can you paste `package.json` exports?
maria: Here is the relevant part we published:```json
"main": "dist/cjs/index.js",
"module": "dist/esm/index.js",
"types": "dist/index.d.ts",
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
  },
  "./package.json": "./package.json"
}
```
olivia: small nit: `types` inside the export target isn't standard for node's conditional exports. Node uses `types` at top-level `types` field (you already have it), but some bundlers read the `types` in exports for TS. Not consistent.
tony: For ESM/CJS parity we usually do an `exports` with `default` and platform-specific conditions. Example that worked for us:```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs",
    "default": "./dist/esm/index.mjs"
  }
}
```
sam: Also consider shipping an explicit `exports` for edge runtimes: many bundlers look for `browser` or `import` condition. Another pattern:```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "browser": "./dist/esm/browser.mjs",
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
  }
}
```
maria: Oh we don't have `browser` variant. We only published `esm` and `cjs`. Could that be the reason?
olivia: Potentially. Cloudflare bundlers sometimes prefer `browser` to avoid pulling Node-specific polyfills. Also watch file extensions: `.mjs` vs `.js` influences loader heuristics. Edge runtimes often choke if transpiled `require()` sneaks in.
tony: Another angle: typings. TS users reported `tsserver` resolving to the CJS `.d.ts` bundle which uses `export =` style instead of ESM `export`. Make sure `types` entry points map to ESM-shaped declarations or provide `.d.ts` per subpath. E.g. add subpath:```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts",
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
  },
  "./*": {
    "types": "./dist/types/*.d.ts"
  }
}
```
sam: TS resolution still prefers `types` field at package root for older TS versions. You can include `typesVersions` in package.json to guide older toolchains.
maria: OK this is getting messy. Proposal: for next publish we ship a tiny `browser`-friendly ESM build (no Node-only bits), change exports order to prefer `import`/`browser` over `require`, and add explicit per-subpath `types`. Also add a CI smoke job that bundles with esbuild targetting Cloudflare and runs a small import test. Thoughts?
olivia: +1 do the smoke job. Also add a Canary tag publish step for `edge` that we can give to customers.
tony: Agreed. One more caution: some consumers still need `require` for CJS projects. Keep the CJS entry but make it a fallback not the primary.
ci-bot: canary published: @redwood/inference@canary-edge (dist: built, exports updated) :rocket:
sam: I'll open a tiny RFC PR with the changed `package.json` and CI steps. Will link real code samples and a repro case.
maria: thanks everyone. If we can get that smoke test green before Monday we can ship a patch and ping the affected customers. :thumbsup:
